<article class="env-lemma" id="08HF">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/08HF">Lemma <span data-tag="08HF">75.13.3</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a scheme. Let $E$ be an object of $D(\mathcal{O}_ X)$. Then </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$E$ has tor amplitude in $[a, b]$ if and only if $\epsilon ^*E$ has tor amplitude in $[a, b]$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$E$ has finite tor dimension if and only if $\epsilon ^*E$ has finite tor dimension. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Here $\epsilon $ is as in (<a href="/tag/08H7" data-tag="08H7">75.4.0.1</a>). </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The easy implication follows from Cohomology on Sites, Lemma <a href="/tag/08H5" data-tag="08H5">21.46.5</a>. For the converse, assume that $\epsilon ^*E$ has tor amplitude in $[a, b]$. Let $\mathcal{F}$ be an $\mathcal{O}_ X$-module. As $\epsilon $ is a flat morphism of ringed sites (Lemma <a href="/tag/08H8" data-tag="08H8">75.4.1</a>) we have 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \epsilon ^*(E \otimes ^\mathbf {L}_{\mathcal{O}_ X} \mathcal{F}) = \epsilon ^*E \otimes ^\mathbf {L}_{\mathcal{O}_{\acute{e}tale}} \epsilon ^*\mathcal{F}  \]
</div>
    <p>
       Thus the (assumed) vanishing of cohomology sheaves on the right hand side implies the desired vanishing of the cohomology sheaves of $E \otimes ^\mathbf {L}_{\mathcal{O}_ X} \mathcal{F}$ via Lemma <a href="/tag/08H8" data-tag="08H8">75.4.1</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>